## The Memory Leak

There are **two compounding bugs**, and together they're brutal:

### 1. Missing dependency array (effect re-runs on every render)

```js
useEffect(() => { ... }); // no [] — runs after every single render
```

Without a dependency array, this effect runs after **every render**, not just once on mount. Since `setScrollY`/`setScrollDirection`/`setLastScrollY` inside the handlers trigger re-renders, and every re-render registers *new* listeners, you get a feedback loop: scroll once → state updates → re-render → effect runs again → two more listeners added → next scroll fires both → two more state updates → four listeners added → ... This grows without bound the longer the user interacts with the page.

### 2. No cleanup function (listeners never removed)

```js
useEffect(() => {
    window.addEventListener('scroll', ...);
    window.addEventListener('resize', ...);
    // no return () => window.removeEventListener(...)
});
```

`useEffect` is supposed to return a cleanup function that undoes what the effect did, so React can call it before the next effect run and before unmount. This effect returns nothing, so nothing is ever removed — from `window`, which lives for the entire browser tab session, independent of whether the component is mounted. Every time `ScrollTracker` mounts (i.e., every time the user navigates back to this page), a fresh event listener is attached to `window` and the old ones from previous mounts are still sitting there, still firing, still holding references to old closures (and thus old versions of the component's state/props) that can never be garbage collected. That's the classic React memory leak signature: "navigate away and back repeatedly → browser gets slower."

### 3. Anonymous inline functions make cleanup impossible even if you tried

```js
window.addEventListener('scroll', () => { ... });
```

`removeEventListener` only removes a listener if you pass a reference **equal** to the one originally passed to `addEventListener`. An arrow function literal created inline produces a brand-new function object every time the effect runs — there is no way to later call `removeEventListener('scroll', <that same function>)` because you never kept a handle to it. Even if someone tried to "fix" this by adding a `return () => window.removeEventListener('scroll', () => {...})`, it would silently fail — that's a *different* anonymous function, not the one that was registered. You must assign the handler to a named variable/function so the same reference can be passed to both `addEventListener` and `removeEventListener`.

## Lifecycle summary

- **Mount** → effect runs → listener(s) added.
- **Re-render** (due to state changes triggered by those very listeners) → because there's no `[]`, the effect runs *again* → *more* listeners added, without removing the old ones.
- **Unmount** (user navigates away) → no cleanup function exists → the listeners attached to `window` are never removed. `window` persists across route changes in an SPA, so they keep firing forever, each one still holding a closure over the old component instance's state setters (which are safe to call but wasteful — React will warn/no-op depending on version) and preventing that old render's closure scope from being garbage collected.
- **Remount** (user navigates back) → the whole cycle starts over, stacking yet more listeners on top of the ones already leaked from last time.

After a few navigations back and forth, you can have dozens of stale `scroll`/`resize` listeners all firing on every pixel of scroll, each doing state calculations and `console.log`s — hence the increasing lag.

## Why React 18 Strict Mode makes this more visible

In development, React 18's `<StrictMode>` intentionally **mounts, unmounts, and remounts** every component once immediately (simulating what happens when a component's state is preserved across a suspend/resume, and to help surface exactly this class of bug). It double-invokes effects: `effect → cleanup → effect` on initial mount. If your cleanup is correct, this is harmless — listeners added, then removed, then added again, net effect: one listener. But because this component has **no cleanup at all**, Strict Mode's double-invocation immediately leaves you with 2 leaked listeners *from the very first mount*, even before the user does anything. This is precisely why the bug should have been caught early in development — Strict Mode is designed to make missing-cleanup bugs like this one loudly obvious rather than something that only shows up after a user clicks around a production app for a while.

## Corrected Code

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function ScrollTracker() {
    const [scrollY, setScrollY] = useState(0);
    const [scrollDirection, setScrollDirection] = useState('none');
    const [lastScrollY, setLastScrollY] = useState(0);

    useEffect(() => {
        // Named handler references so the *same* function reference
        // can be passed to both addEventListener and removeEventListener.
        function handleScroll() {
            const currentScrollY = window.scrollY;
            // Use functional form so we don't need `lastScrollY` in the
            // dependency array (avoids re-subscribing on every scroll).
            setLastScrollY((prevLastScrollY) => {
                setScrollDirection(currentScrollY > prevLastScrollY ? 'down' : 'up');
                return currentScrollY;
            });
            setScrollY(currentScrollY);
        }

        function handleResize() {
            console.log('Window resized, recalculating...');
            setScrollY(window.scrollY);
        }

        window.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
        window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);

        // Cleanup: remove the exact same references that were added.
        return () => {
            window.removeEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
            window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
        };
    }, []); // empty array: run once on mount, clean up once on unmount

    return (
        <div style={{ position: 'fixed', top: 10, right: 10, padding: '10px', background: '#333', color: '#fff' }}>
            <p>Scroll Y: {scrollY}</p>
            <p>Direction: {scrollDirection}</p>
        </div>
    );
}

export default ScrollTracker;
```

### Key fixes and why they preserve behavior

- **`[]` dependency array**: The effect now runs exactly once on mount and cleans up exactly once on unmount (Strict Mode will do mount→cleanup→mount in dev, which now correctly nets to one pair of listeners — proving the fix works).
- **Named `handleScroll`/`handleResize` functions**: Same function reference used for both `addEventListener` and `removeEventListener`, so cleanup actually removes what was added.
- **`return () => { ...removeEventListener... }`**: Runs on unmount (and before any effect re-run, though that no longer happens here), guaranteeing no listener outlives the component.
- **Functional update `setLastScrollY(prev => ...)`**: Since the effect no longer re-runs on every render, `handleScroll` is created once and closes over the *initial* `lastScrollY` (stale closure problem) if we referenced `lastScrollY` directly. Using the functional updater form lets us read the latest value from React's state queue instead of the closure, so direction detection still works correctly on every scroll event — same functionality as the original, just without needing `lastScrollY` (or `scrollDirection`) in the dependency array.
- Functionality is otherwise identical: same state (`scrollY`, `scrollDirection`), same rendering, same console log on resize.

